speechtext:
In pursuance of the understanding, notice of which has already been given, it becomes necessary now to move amendments to the resolutions as brought down. Before doing so, I desire to point out first that if hon. members will look at the list, they will find that paragraph (a) of the first resolution mentions boots, men's clothing, men's overcoats, cloth overcoats, waistcoats, women's dresses, women's suits. All articles of clothing, either boots or clothes, are contained in the list. Then, if hon. gentlemen will look at paragraph (b), they will find an item of carpets, the provision there being that carpets in excess of S3 per yard are subject to the tax provided in the paragraph. The suggestions embodied in Hansard provide for the removal of those articles from the provisions which impose a direct tax on the whole of the amount, when the amount is in excess of the sum stated, into a clause where they will be taxed only on the amount in excess of 'the amount stated. Therefore, I move:
That the following words be struck out of paragraphs (a) and (b) of section 1:
And on articles of clothing the said tax may be imposed upon the combined selling price of materials and cost of manufacture when sold separately:-;
Boots, shoes, pumps and slippers of any material (not including shoes or appliances made to order for persons having a crippled or deformed foot or ankle) in excess of $9 per pair;
Clothing consisting of coat, vest and pants or coat and pants, men's and boy's, in excess of $45;
Cloth overcoats, men's and iboy's, women's and misses', in excess of $50 each ;
Waistcoats, men's, sold separately from suits, in excess of $5 each ;
Dresses, women's and misses', in excess of $45 each ;
Suits, women's and misses', in excess of $60 each ;
Carpets in excess of $3 per yard ;
Knitted sweaters and knitted sweater coats in excess of $15 ; and the following paragraphs (d) be inserted after paragraph (c) :

(d) That the following excise taxes be imposed, levied and collected on so much of the amount paid for any of the following articles as is in excess of the price hereinafter specified as to each such article:
Hon. gentlemen will see that we are taxing now merely the excess in so far as these particular items are concerned, carrying out the proposition made when the matter was last under consideration:
A tax of 10 per cent on:
Carpets and rugs in excess of $6 per linear yard of 27 inches in width;
A tax of 15 per cent on,-
Boots, shoes, pumps and slippers of any material (not including shoes or appliances made to order for persons having a crippled or deformed foot or ankle, or to top boots not less than twelve inches in height such as are used in lumbering, mining and fishing industries, or to river driving boots fitted with caulks on soles thereof) in excess of $9 per pair;
Clothing consisting of coat, vest and pants, or coat and pants, men's and boy's, in excess of $45.

Yes, it is merely transferring it from the straight 10 per cent tax on the whole, to 15 per cent on the excess, a much lower tax.
Trousers, sold separately from suits in excess of $12 per pair;
Coats, men's and boy's, sold separtely from suits, (not including leather coats lined with sheepskin) in excess of $25 each;
Cloth overcoats, men's, boy's, women's and misses', in excess of $50 each ;
Waistcoats, men's, sold separately from suits, in excess of $45 each;
Dresses, women's and misses', except silk, in excess of $45 each;
Skirts, separate from dresses, except silk, in excess of $15 each;
Suits, women's and misses', except silk, in excess of $60 each :
Coats, women's and misses', sold separately from suits, except silk, in excess of $35 each;
On articles of clothing, the selling price of materials and cost of manufacture when sold separately are to be combined when determining the selling price.	[DOT]
Mr. MeMASTER:	Has the minister any
printed copies of what he is reading?

The item now reads:
Pyjamas in excess of ?5 per pair ;
Underwear consisting of shirts and drawers in excess of $4 per garment ;
Underwear, combinations, in excess of $8 each;
Item, as amended, agreed to.
Purses and pocket-books, in excess of ?2 each ;

That is covered by the proposal made the other day. I move:
That the words "coats and robes, the component material of chief value being fur, in excess of $100- each", in paragraph (a), and the words "wearing apparel, not otherwise provided for in this resolution, the component material of chief value being fur," in paragraph (b), be stricken out, and the following words be inserted in paragraph (a) :
Coats, the component material of chief value being fur, including repairs thereto, in excess of $200 each ;
Gloves, the component material of chief value being furT in excess of $1'5 per pair;
Caps, the component material of chief value being fur, in excess of $15 per pair;
Muffs and neckpieces, the component material of chief value being fur, in excess of $35 each;
Robes and rugs, the component material of chief value being fur, in excess of $50 each;
Wearing apparel, not elsewhere specified, the component material of chief value being fur.

I am a very economical person and rarely buy these goods, but I have seen, for instance, beautifully coloured Venetian glassware. You can buy it in Venice when you go there, if you have enough money. I know there are other kinds of glassware. I cannot specify them at the present moment, but I believe if the minister went into an establishment such as Henry Birks and Sons, Montreal, he would see not only this Venetian glassware but other beautiful and very expensive glassware which are neither cut glassware nor etched glassware.
Item las amended agreed to.
Sporting goods, such as tennis rackets, nets, racket covers and presses, skates, snowshoes, skis, toboggans, canoe paddles and cushions, polo mallets, baseball bats, gloves, masks, protectors, shoes and uniforms, football helmets, harness and goals, basket-ball goals and uniforms, golf bags and clubs, lacrosse sticks, balls of all kinds, fishing rods and reels, billiard and pool tables, chess and checker boards and pieces, dice, games and parts of games (except playing cards' and children's toys' and games, all similar articles commonly or commercially known as sporting goods in exoess of 50' cents ;

I beg to move
that this item be struck out and that the following be substituted therefor:
Sporting goods, such as tennis rackets, nets, racket covers and presses, snowshoes, skis, canoe paddles and cushions, polo mallets, baseball protectors, football helmets, harness and goals, basketball goals and uniforms, golf bags and clubs, baseball, laorosse, hockey and football uniforms, balls of all kinds not hereinafter specified (not including balls for children) fishing rods, reels, lines, spoons and artificial bait, billiard and pool tables, chess andi checker boards and pieces, dice, games, and parts of games (except playing cards and children's toys, games and express wagons) and all similar articles not elsewhere specified commonly or commercially known as sporting goods in excess of 50 cents ;
Baseball bats and baseballs in excess of $1.25 ;
Baseball masks and gloves in excess of $1.50 ;
Skates in excess of $2.00 per pair;
Toboggans and hand sleds in excess of $3.00 each ;
Footballs in excess of $3.00 each;
Lacrosse sticks in excess of $1.'5'0 each;
Hockey sticks in excess of 75 cents each.
